<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/advanced/international-trade">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Trade economics rests on one of the oldest, most-verified and most-misunderstood results:
    <strong>comparative advantage</strong>. Countries gain by specialising where they are
    <em>relatively</em> best — not absolutely best — and trading. Global trade has lifted
    hundreds of millions out of poverty since 1990. It has also concentrated losses on specific
    workers and regions, and "the gains exist" is not an argument that the gains were shared.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Ricardo's logic, modern evidence, and the honest ledger" widget="equationStep" config={tradeCfg} />

  <h2>The modern debates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Factor endowments to global value chains:</strong> a phone is "made" in 40 countries — trade statistics attribute full value to the last assembler, distorting politics (iPhone economics research, Xing & Detert).</li>
    <li><strong>Infant industries & industrial policy:</strong> South Korea, Taiwan and China grew behind protection before competing globally. Whether that validates protection depends on picking winners after the fact — economists remain split.</li>
    <li><strong>Sanctions and weaponised trade:</strong> interdependence is leverage. Energy and chip export controls turned trade theory into geopolitics — a field economists used to leave to the strategists.</li>
    <li><strong>The consensus position:</strong> trade's aggregate gains are large and real; its costs are concentrated, persistent and politically explosive. Compensation (retraining, regional investment) is the missing half of the textbook case.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ricardo, Principles (1817); Autor, Dorn & Hanson (2013); Krugman (Nobel 2008) on new trade theory.</p>
</TopicPage>
